{% extends "base.jinja" %}
{% block body %}
    <div class="container nt-about">
        <h2>About newtrackon.co.nz</h2>
<p>A Wellington, New Zealand fork of newTrackon: it checks public BitTorrent trackers around the clock and lists the ones that
really work. For tracker lists to paste into your client, see the <a href="/list">List</a> or <a href="/api">API</a> pages.
Each tracker has its own page (the <i class="fa-solid fa-chart-line" style="color:#c15cff"></i> icon) with its uptime, latency history and score;
tracker operators can see what's wrong and how to fix it on <a href="/fix">How to fix</a>.
<a href="/rankings">Rankings</a> shows who's been best lately, and <a href="/tools">Tools</a> adds working trackers to a magnet link or .torrent.</p>
{% set hl = nt_health() %}<div class="nt-health" style="font-size:.85rem;opacity:.75;margin:0 0 12px">{% if not hl.online %}<span style="color:#ffa500">&#9888; Monitor offline: checks are paused until its connection is back, and nothing is counted against trackers meanwhile.</span>{% else %}Monitor: {{ hl.checks_hour }} check{{ "" if hl.checks_hour == 1 else "s" }} in the last hour{% if hl.last_ago %} &middot; last {{ hl.last_ago }}{% endif %} &middot; latency from {{ hl.regions|join(", ") if hl.regions else "this server only" }}{% endif %}</div>

<h4>What a check tests</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>It answers</b>: a real announce over UDP, HTTP or HTTPS, the same request a torrent client sends.</li>
<li><b>It hands out peers</b>: a second test client, from a different country, must be told about the first one.</li>
<li><b>No fake peers</b>: the test torrent is a random one only this site knows, so any other peer in the reply is fake.</li>
<li><b>Every published address works</b>: IPv4 and IPv6 are each tested, from here and from abroad.</li>
<li><b>Speed</b>: measured from four places: Oceania, Asia, Europe and North America.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a check fails because of <em>our</em> connection rather than the tracker, nothing is recorded.</p>

<h4>The score (0&ndash;100)</h4>
<p><b>Availability &times; stability</b>, weighted towards the last week. A single outage costs little; constant up-and-down is punished
hard. Slow trackers lose a few points (none under 150&nbsp;ms, &minus;10 at 650&nbsp;ms, at most &minus;40). New trackers start
capped at 80 and earn up to 100 over their first 7 days.</p>
<table class="table table-sm" style="max-width:520px">
<thead><tr><th>Over two weeks</th><th>Score</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Up the whole time</td><td>100</td></tr>
<tr><td>One 6-hour outage</td><td>96</td></tr>
<tr><td>Down half an hour in every 5 hours</td><td>57</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flapping every hour</td><td>13</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Handing out no peers or fake peers sets the score to <b>0</b>; a dead IPv4 or IPv6 address caps it at <b>50</b>. Penalties lift as soon as the
tracker behaves.</p>

<h4>Statuses</h4>
<ul>
<li><b style="color:#00e676">Up/Good</b>: passes everything, score 90+.</li>
<li><b>Up/Slow</b>: reliable, but slow (300&nbsp;ms or more on average).</li>
<li><b>Up/Unreliable</b>: under 90 because of dropouts. <b>Up/Junk</b>: under 50.</li>
<li><b>Up/New</b>: healthy, still building its first week of history.</li>
<li><b>Up/Bad</b>: answers but fails a test (no peers, or fake peers). <b>Up/Broken</b>: one of its IPv4/IPv6 addresses is dead.</li>
<li><b style="color:#e0e0e0">Down</b> &#129702;: not answering, with how long.</li>
</ul>

<h4>How often</h4>
<p>Every 15&nbsp;minutes for trackers that just failed, 30&ndash;60&nbsp;minutes normally, up to 4&nbsp;hours for long-proven ones (and for long-dead
ones). A tracker's own announce interval doesn't matter.</p>

<h4>Removal</h4>
<p>After 3+ days listed, a tracker is removed if it hasn't answered for 5 days, or is up too rarely to be useful. It can't be resubmitted for 30 days.</p>

<h4>Credits</h4>
<p>newTrackon is by <a href="https://github.com/CorralPeltzer">Miguel &Aacute;ngel Corral (CorralPeltzer)</a>
(<a href="https://github.com/CorralPeltzer/newTrackon">GitHub</a>), based on <a href="http://repo.cat-v.org/trackon/">Trackon</a> by
<a href="http://uriel.cat-v.org/">Uriel &dagger;</a>, with a scraper by <a href="https://github.com/erindru">Erin Drummond</a>.
The NZ changes (testing, scoring, scheduling) are by <b>Kolan Koala</b>; their code is on
<a href="https://github.com/KolanTheKoala/newtrackon-nz">GitHub</a>.</p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
{% set title = "About - newTrackon NZ" %}
